  quiz: [
    {q:"An insured intentionally jumps from a 4-foot wall and breaks his leg. He meant to jump but did not mean to be hurt. Under the definition Utah requires in an accident benefit, the injury is:", c:["Covered, because only the result has to be unintended","Not covered, because the cause of the injury was intentional","Covered only if there is an external, visible wound","Not covered, because jumping is a hazardous activity"], a:0, w:"Utah requires accident benefits to use results language and bans the accidental-means test (R590-126-6(15)), so only the result (the broken leg) must be unintended. 'Cause was intentional' is the accidental-means answer, which Utah prohibits; 'external, visible wound' wording is also banned.", o:"9.1", ut:true},
    {q:"Which statement best describes the difference between accident and sickness coverage?", c:["Sickness is the costlier peril to insure, so many limited policies cover accident only","Sickness coverage is cheaper because illness is more predictable","Accident coverage pays for illness that results from an injury","Accident policies must include a probationary period"], a:0, w:"Sickness claims are more frequent and expensive, which is why accident-only policies are common and cheaper. Accident policies may not have any probationary period in Utah (R590-126-4(1)).", o:"9.1", ut:false},
    {q:"A policy pays $150 for each day the insured is hospitalized, regardless of the actual bill. This is an example of:", c:["A service benefit","An expense-incurred benefit","A reimbursement benefit","An indemnity benefit"], a:3, w:"An indemnity (valued) benefit pays a fixed amount when the loss occurs. Expense-incurred and reimbursement mean the same thing: paying actual charges. Service benefits pay the provider for services, as Blues and HMOs do.", o:"9.2", ut:false},
    {q:"Under an AD&D benefit, the insured loses one hand in a covered accident. The policy will most likely pay:", c:["The principal sum","The capital sum, a percentage of the principal sum","Nothing, because dismemberment requires losing two limbs","Only the medical expenses for the amputation"], a:1, w:"The capital sum (often half) is paid for a single limb or eye. The principal sum is paid for death or double dismemberment.", o:"9.2", ut:false},
    {q:"Under Utah rules, an accidental death and dismemberment benefit must be payable if the loss occurs within how many days of the accident?", c:["90 days","180 days","365 days","30 days"], a:1, w:"R590-126-5(11) requires AD&D to be payable if the loss occurs within 180 days of the accident. 90 days is a common older policy limit but is shorter than Utah allows.", o:"9.2", ut:true},
    {q:"An individual health policy lets the insurer raise premiums for the whole class but never refuse to renew before age 65. The policy is:", c:["Noncancellable","Optionally renewable","Guaranteed renewable","Conditionally renewable"], a:2, w:"Guaranteed renewable: no right to refuse renewal, but premiums can change by class. Noncancellable also locks the premium. Conditionally renewable allows nonrenewal by class or stated reasons, and optionally renewable lets the insurer decline renewal at the anniversary.", o:"9.3", ut:true},
    {q:"In Utah, an insurer that decides not to renew an individual A&H contract must give the insured at least how much notice?", c:["30 days","45 days","60 days","90 days"], a:3, w:"R590-126-5(3)(f) requires at least 90 days' notice of nonrenewal. 45 days is the premium-change notice requirement, the tempting wrong answer.", o:"9.3", ut:true},
    {q:"Under Utah standards, an accident-only policy must provide at least which death benefit?", c:["$1,000","$500","$5,000","$10,000"], a:0, w:"R590-126-7(7) sets minimums of $1,000 for death, $1,000 for double dismemberment and $500 for single dismemberment. $500 is the single-dismemberment minimum, not the death minimum.", o:"9.4", ut:true},
    {q:"A Utah hospital fixed indemnity policy must pay at least:", c:["$50 a day for at least 31 days per period of confinement","$100 a day for 365 days","80% of the semiprivate room rate","$25 a day for 90 days"], a:0, w:"R590-126-7(5): at least $50 a day for at least 31 days per confinement, paid regardless of other insurance. The 80% of semiprivate figure is the basic hospital expense standard.", o:"9.4", ut:true},
    {q:"When must a producer give an applicant the buyer's guide for a specified disease (cancer) policy in Utah?", c:["At policy delivery","Within 30 days after issue","Only if the applicant asks","At the time of application, with a signed acknowledgment"], a:3, w:"R590-126-6(11): a non-direct-response insurer gives the buyer's guide at application and obtains written acknowledgment. Only a direct response insurer may provide it on request, by delivery.", o:"9.4", ut:true},
    {q:"All of the following are true of specified disease insurance in Utah EXCEPT:", c:["Benefits are paid regardless of other coverage","It may be built directly into a whole life policy as a policy provision","It can't be more restrictive than guaranteed renewable","The application must say Medicaid recipients are not eligible"], a:1, w:"Specified disease coverage sold with life insurance or an annuity must be a separate endorsement and may not be incorporated into the life or annuity contract (R590-126-5(15)). The other three are Utah requirements.", o:"9.4", ut:true},
    {q:"Which exclusion is NOT permitted in a Utah individual A&H policy?", c:["Injury while serving in the armed forces","Injury while a fare-paying passenger on a commercial airline","Intentionally self-inflicted injury","Cosmetic surgery that isn't reconstructive"], a:1, w:"Utah allows an aviation exclusion only for non-fare-paying passengers, such as private pilots and crew (R590-126-4(2)(f)). Military service, self-inflicted injury and non-reconstructive cosmetic surgery are on the approved exclusion list.", o:"9.5", ut:true},
    {q:"A 17-year-old insured is hurt while driving with a blood-alcohol level over the legal limit. Can her Utah A&H policy deny the claim under its DUI exclusion?", c:["Yes, if the insurer's own claims adjuster concludes she was over the limit","Yes, once she is convicted","No, because the DUI exclusion never applies to an insured under age 18","No, because Utah prohibits all alcohol-related exclusions"], a:2, w:"The Utah DUI exclusion requires proof by criminal finding, plea, diversion or independent review, and it does not apply to an insured under 18 (R590-126-4(3)(c)). Utah does permit the exclusion for adults, so 'prohibits all' is wrong.", o:"9.5", ut:true},
    {q:"Which of the following is considered a complication of pregnancy that a Utah policy may NOT exclude when it excludes normal pregnancy?", c:["Morning sickness","False labor","Ectopic pregnancy","Doctor-prescribed bed rest"], a:2, w:"Pregnancy may be excluded except complications of pregnancy. A terminated ectopic pregnancy is a listed complication; morning sickness, false labor and prescribed rest are specifically not complications (R590-126-3).", o:"9.5", ut:true},
    {q:"Under R590-126, a limited A&H policy may impose a probationary period of up to six months for which condition?", c:["Hernia","Heart disease","Cancer","Broken bones from an accident"], a:0, w:"The six-month list is hernia, adenoids, tonsils, varicose veins, appendix and reproductive-organ disorders (HAT VAR). Accident coverage may not have any probationary period.", o:"9.5", ut:true},
    {q:"An insurer's application asks no health questions. Under Utah law, when must it begin covering a loss from a preexisting condition that the policy does not specifically exclude?", c:["Immediately","After 6 months","After 24 months","For losses occurring more than 12 months after the effective date"], a:3, w:"31A-22-605.1(2): with no health questions, non-excluded preexisting conditions are covered for losses more than 12 months after the effective date. Six months is the specified-disease limit; 24 months is the look-back in the definition of a preexisting condition.", o:"9.5", ut:true},
    {q:"A producer completes an individual health application and the applicant asks him to correct an answer. The producer should:", c:["Change the answer and initial it himself","Leave the error and explain it to the underwriter by phone","Make the correction and have the applicant initial it","Start a new application only if the premium changes"], a:2, w:"Changes must be initialed by the applicant, whose signed statements become part of the contract. A producer initialing the change alone is improper.", o:"9.6", ut:false},
    {q:"An individual A&H policy is issued with an exclusion rider that changes the coverage from what was applied for. Under Utah law, what must accompany the policy at delivery?", c:["A new outline of coverage clearly stating it is not the policy applied for","A replacement notice","The specified disease buyer's guide","Nothing; the producer explains the change orally"], a:0, w:"31A-22-605(7) requires a new outline when a policy is issued on a basis other than applied for, stating that it is not the coverage applied for. A replacement notice is only for replacements.", o:"9.6", ut:true},
    {q:"Which is NOT a typical source of information an insurer uses in individual health underwriting?", c:["Medical Information Bureau report","Attending physician statement","Inspection report","The applicant's genetic test results, used to set premiums"], a:3, w:"Federal GINA bars health insurers from using genetic information for eligibility or premiums. MIB reports, APSs and inspection reports are standard sources.", o:"9.7", ut:false},
    {q:"An applicant is offered a policy with a higher premium and a rider excluding her back condition. She has been classified as:", c:["Standard","Preferred","Substandard","Declined"], a:2, w:"Substandard risks are issued with an extra premium, an exclusion rider or reduced benefits. Standard and preferred risks pay normal or lower rates without restrictions.", o:"9.7", ut:false},
    {q:"Which statement about underwriting individual major medical coverage after the ACA is correct?", c:["Insurers may decline applicants with serious conditions","Individual major medical is guaranteed issue with no preexisting-condition exclusions","Insurers may add exclusion riders for chronic conditions","Underwriting applies but only for applicants over 50"], a:1, w:"The ACA makes individual major medical guaranteed issue and bans preexisting exclusions. Health underwriting still matters for disability income and limited or excepted-benefit policies.", o:"9.7", ut:false},
    {q:"A producer learns a client will drop an existing individual health policy when the new one is issued. Under Utah rules, the producer must:", c:["Notify the existing insurer within 3 business days","Give the client the replacement notice before the policy is issued or delivered, with the insurer keeping a copy","Give the replacement notice within 30 days after delivery","Do nothing, because replacement rules apply only to life insurance"], a:1, w:"R590-126-9 and R590-233-9 require the replacement notice before issuance or delivery, signed by the applicant, with the insurer retaining a copy. Notifying the existing insurer is a life-insurance replacement concept; A&H replacement rules clearly apply.", o:"9.8", ut:true},
    {q:"The Utah A&H replacement notice warns the applicant mainly about:", c:["Losing cash values","Surrender charges on the old policy","Preexisting conditions not being immediately or fully covered under the new policy","Higher commissions paid on new policies"], a:2, w:"The prescribed notice focuses on preexisting conditions, the right to consult the present insurer, and answering health questions truthfully. Cash values and surrender charges are life insurance replacement concerns.", o:"9.8", ut:true},
    {q:"Under the direct-response A&H replacement notice in Utah, how long does the buyer have to decide without cost whether to keep the policy?", c:["10 days","20 days","45 days","30 days"], a:3, w:"The direct-response notice (R590-126-9 Table 13) gives 30 days to decide and asks the buyer to write within 10 days if the application is incorrect. Don't confuse the 10 days to correct the application with the 30-day look period.", o:"9.8", ut:true},
    {q:"An insured pays a monthly premium on an individual Utah A&H policy. What is the minimum grace period?", c:["7 days","10 days","15 days","31 days"], a:2, w:"31A-22-607 requires at least 15 days for weekly or monthly premium policies and 30 days for other modes. 7/10/31 are the national NAIC model numbers, which Utah law has replaced.", o:"9.9", ut:true},
    {q:"Under Utah law, how long after an individual A&H policy has been in force can the insurer no longer contest it for misstatements in the application, other than fraud?", c:["1 year","2 years","3 years","It can always contest for any misstatement"], a:1, w:"31A-22-609 sets 2 years and lets the insurer contest fraud only by clear and convincing evidence. Three years is the NAIC model figure.", o:"9.9", ut:true},
    {q:"A Utah policyholder lets an individual A&H policy lapse and applies for reinstatement. The insurer issues a conditional receipt and says nothing. The policy is automatically reinstated after:", c:["10 days","30 days","45 days","60 days"], a:2, w:"31A-22-608: reinstated on the 45th day after the conditional receipt unless disapproved in writing. Ten days is the sickness waiting period after reinstatement; 60 days is the limit on back premium.", o:"9.9", ut:true},
    {q:"Under Utah law, an A&H policy may not require written proof of loss sooner than how long after the date of loss?", c:["20 days","90 days","1 year","180 days"], a:3, w:"31A-22-614(3) sets 180 days. Twenty days is the notice-of-claim period, and 90 days is the national NAIC model figure.", o:"9.9", ut:true},
    {q:"An insured disagrees with a claim denial. Under Utah law, when may she sue the insurer?", c:["Immediately after filing notice of claim","Only after 60 days from furnishing proof of loss, unless the claim is denied sooner, and within 3 years after the loss","Only after 1 year from the loss","Within 2 years of policy issue"], a:1, w:"31A-21-313: no suit until 60 days after proof of loss (or an earlier denial), and suit must start within 3 years after the loss. A denial lets an A&H insured sue without waiting the full 60 days.", o:"9.9", ut:true},
    {q:"A policyholder changes from office work to a much more hazardous occupation and doesn't tell the insurer. When she files a disability claim, the insurer will:", c:["Deny the claim entirely","Cancel the policy and refund premium","Reduce benefits to what her premium would buy at the riskier occupation's rate","Pay full benefits and bill the extra premium"], a:2, w:"The change of occupation provision reduces benefits proportionately when the change is to a riskier job (31A-22-613(1)). A move to a safer job lowers the premium instead.", o:"9.9", ut:true},
    {q:"Which is an OPTIONAL uniform provision rather than a mandatory one?", c:["Grace period","Relation of earnings to insurance","Proof of loss","Physical examination and autopsy"], a:1, w:"Relation of earnings to insurance is one of the 11 optional provisions (it guards against overinsurance in disability policies). Grace period, proof of loss and physical exam are among the 12 mandatory provisions.", o:"9.9", ut:false},
    {q:"A couple's family health policy covers a newborn. When does the baby's coverage begin under Utah law?", c:["From the moment of birth, including congenital defects","After 30 days","After the hospital discharge","Only after the baby passes underwriting"], a:0, w:"31A-22-610 covers a newborn from the moment of birth, including congenital defects and prematurity, without preexisting limits. The 30 days is the deadline to enroll the child if an extra premium is required, not a waiting period.", o:"9.9", ut:true}
  ]
